"updated_at": 1789858731.843341, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "DATE: 2026-09-19  \nSESSION: EC⁸ Edge Crew Quant — NCAA Data Package Review  \nSEAT: Coda — Strategic Advisor / Governance  \nCURRENT GATE: Package Assessment\n\nThe **good is very good**: Luchi/Falcon proved that the NCAA data concept works. The package covers essentially the entire analytical surface we defined—team/game performance, players, QBs, OL, defensive front, receivers, rushing, special teams, roster continuity, coaching/scheme, injuries, situational factors, venue/weather, history, market, drives, provenance, and gaps. fileciteturn0file1\n\nMore importantly, the package obeyed the architecture. **Market is separated from football evidence. Missing data is exposed rather than invented. Conflicts are preserved. Provenance is attached.** That is exactly how Edge Crew data should behave. fileciteturn0file1\n\n### The good\n\n**1. We can actually populate the NCAA framework.**  \nLSU–Ole Miss has enough evidence across virtually every proposed component to run the schema-driven analysis we did earlier from a much cleaner structured base. fileciteturn0file2\n\n**2. NCAA-specific continuity is captured.**  \nThis is a major win. We have coaching changes, staff migration, transfers, returning personnel, OL continuity, QB history, and recent head-to-head context. That means we can implement the principle we discovered:\n\n> Historical relevance depends on present-day continuity.\n\n**3. QB state is much richer than basic passing stats.**  \nLeavitt's performance, rushing, interceptions, experience, availability timeline, and injury-source conflict are represented. Chambliss has both 2026 data and meaningful 2025 continuity. fileciteturn0file2\n\n**4. Data Hound did not become the analyst.**  \nThis matters a lot. Falcon did **not** assign weights, fair lines, edges, EV, or picks. It supplied evidence. That's the correct seat boundary. fileciteturn0file0\n\n**5. Provenance is becoming real infrastructure rather than an afterthought.**  \nWe now have a source registry and quality flags. This directly addresses the conversation we just had about “where is this actually coming from?”\n\n### The bad\n\nThe biggest weakness isn't lack of broad data. It's that **the highest-value advanced fields are exactly where the package gets thin.**\n\nWe still do not have reliable full-game:\n\n- play-level data / EPA\n- opponent-adjusted efficiency\n- pressure and pressure-allowed detail\n- pass-defense charting\n- some target/participation detail\n- complete special-teams information for LSU\n- exact Ole Miss OL five\n- sufficiently granular market history\n\nThose are explicitly identified as gaps. fileciteturn0file0\n\nThat means we currently have a very strong **descriptive football package**, but we're not yet at the point where every component has the advanced quantitative inputs needed for a mature predictive engine.\n\nThe second problem is **data conflicts**. There are seven already: Lacy's 2025 rushing totals, the Louisiana Tech final, Beavers' status, series record, kickoff representation, Leavitt injury characterization, and Ole Miss ranking depending on poll. fileciteturn0file0\n\nI'm actually happy those conflicts are there because Falcon handled them correctly. But it demonstrates why we need a formal source hierarchy eventually. Right now we know two sources disagree; later we need rules such as:\n\n**official game book > official team release > primary data provider > wire service > secondary media**, with sport/field-specific exceptions.\n\nThe third issue is **opponent adjustment**.\n\nThis is probably the biggest methodology gap exposed by the package. Falcon correctly refused to invent it. fileciteturn0file0\n\nBut NCAA absolutely needs it. Otherwise:\n\n**41 points against Charlotte**  \nand  \n**41 points against Louisville**\n\ncan enter the engine as superficially equivalent production.\n\nThey're not.\n\nSo we have the underlying observations, but we haven't defined the transformation that says **how much should each performance count given opponent quality?**\n\n### And then there's one technical bad\n\nThe earlier CSV ingestion showed that several flat files have serialization/schema issues—fields spilling because of complex text/comma content and inconsistent row lengths.\n\nThat's fixable.\n\nIt means:\n\n> **The information model is stronger than the current file format.**\n\nSome of this probably wants normalized tables or JSON rather than stuffing complex notes into CSV cells.\n\nI would absolutely **not redesign the methodology because of that**. We fix the transport/schema.\n\n### My assessment\n\nIf I separate it cleanly:\n\n**Evidence coverage: A-/B+**  \nVery good for a first NCAA contract test.\n\n**Provenance discipline: A**  \nExactly the behavior we want.\n\n**Advanced predictive data: C+/B-**  \nSeveral of the most valuable advanced fields remain unavailable/partial.\n\n**Schema stability: C right now**  \nGood conceptual entities; serialization needs hardening.\n\n**Methodology readiness: B-**  \nEnough to continue discovery and run games. Not enough yet to lock weights or claim a mature NCAA predictive engine.\n\nAnd there is one thing I would **not do yet**: build more data just because we can.\n\nThe next intelligent move is to take LSU–Ole Miss and ask:\n\n> **For every input we already have, where does it actually enter our 13 components and ultimately affect the calculation?**\n\nAnything we collected that has no analytical destination is probably noise.\n\nAnything the model needs that has no data source becomes a true gap.\n\nThat gives us the **NCAA Data Contract v0.1**, rather than simply a larger pile of files.\n\nSTATUS: **CHECKPOINT**  \nCONFIDENCE: **98%**  \nREASON: Acquisition architecture passed its first real contract test; the remaining problems are now specific—advanced metrics, opponent adjustment, conflict resolution, and schema normalization—rather than vague “we need more data.”  \nNEXT GATE: **Map the LSU–Ole Miss package field-by-field into the NCAA component model and identify required vs optional data.**  \nDON INPUT NEEDED: **No — this is Coda reconciliation work.**", "text_chars": 6045, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "ef73b1fc-8184-4fca-9aae-599d8896e389", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789858711.216056, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "666e8eb1-326f-498d-ac0e-6a642150d33f", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789858712.6949363, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "845b33fe-c268-46a1-811b-55b49af4d557", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789858713.466738, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "6dfe8245-028e-4b93-8e6f-036648cdfbd6", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789858810.9364371, "updated_at": 1789858863.193478, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah, take your time, do the reconciliation. I read that all. Really good. Now, I want you and I to be smart about this, right? If I gotta slow down and you gotta slow down, we slow down. Because this is so important on a data layer. If there's any gaps, let's pause and say, Peter, we probably need an API for this, or Peter, let's try this, right? The goal is to get to 99% data, right? For every sport, right? If it's not there, then we pause and say, okay, maybe we just strip this from our, from the methodology.", "text_chars": 517, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "5d73cc09-8629-412e-88d5-cc3d303b5d56", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789858822.671043, "updated_at": 1789858862.236613, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "DATE: 2026-09-19  \nSESSION: EC⁸ Edge Crew Quant — NCAA Data Reconciliation  \nSEAT: Coda — Strategic Advisor / Governance  \nCURRENT GATE: Data Sufficiency / Methodology Retention\n\nThat is exactly the posture I want us to take.\n\nThe governing rule should be:\n\n> **We do not keep a methodology variable merely because it sounds analytically useful. If we cannot source it reliably enough for production, we either acquire it properly, derive it transparently, downgrade it, or remove it.**\n\nThat is the discipline that gets us toward 99% instead of building a model full of elegant-looking holes.\n\nAfter reconciling the package against the framework, I would separate NCAA data into four buckets.\n\n### 1. KEEP — data coverage is already good enough\n\nThese are strong enough to remain in the methodology now:\n\n**Core team performance**\n- scoring\n- yards/play\n- run/pass production\n- third down\n- red zone\n- turnovers\n- sacks/TFL\n- basic explosive production\n\n**QB baseline**\n- attempts/completions\n- yards\n- TD/INT\n- rushing\n- experience\n- prior-season continuity\n- availability/injury state\n\n**Roster/continuity**\n- transfers\n- returning players/starters\n- coaching movement\n- position-group continuity\n- staff overlap\n\n**Coaching/scheme**\n- head coach\n- coordinators\n- tenure\n- system continuity\n- broad scheme identity\n\n**Situational/environment**\n- schedule\n- home/away/neutral\n- rest\n- venue\n- weather\n- historical matchup\n- market snapshots kept separately\n\nThe current package proves we can populate those consistently enough to build around them. fileciteturn2file6\n\n### 2. KEEP, BUT UPGRADE SOURCE — likely API/licensed-feed territory\n\nThese matter enough that I do **not** want to strip them yet.\n\n**Play-by-play / drive-level data**\n\nThis is the clearest one.\n\nRight now we only have aggregate EPA/success/explosive information for selected games, while actual row-level PBP is unavailable. Falcon explicitly identifies CFBD/PBP subscription as the likely path. fileciteturn2file4\n\nI would say:\n\n**PAUSE. Investigate an API/feed.**\n\nBecause with play-level data we can internally derive a huge amount:\n\n- EPA/play\n- success rate\n- early-down success\n- passing downs\n- explosiveness\n- garbage-time filters\n- drive efficiency\n- field position\n- game-state effects\n- finishing drives\n- opponent-adjusted inputs later\n\nOne paid feed may solve ten methodology gaps.\n\nThat is economically rational.\n\n**Pressure / pass protection / charting**\n\nThe current OL file gives us starters, experience and sacks allowed, but not stable pressure-allowed or blocking-level data. fileciteturn1file6\n\nReceivers are even thinner: target shares, targets, YAC and route-level information are largely partial. fileciteturn2file3\n\nBefore stripping those concepts, I want to know what providers can give us reliably.\n\nLikely candidates for investigation:\n- pressure rate\n- pressure allowed\n- QB pressure splits\n- route participation\n- target share\n- missed tackles\n- pass-defense targeting\n- snap participation\n\nThese are worth paying for **if they materially improve the model and coverage is genuinely FBS-wide**.\n\n### 3. DERIVE INTERNALLY — do not pay somebody for the conclusion\n\nThis is where I think we can save a lot.\n\n**Opponent adjustment** is currently UNKNOWN because Falcon correctly did not invent a method. fileciteturn1file2\n\nBut I do **not** initially view opponent adjustment as “buy an API field.”\n\nI view it as:\n\n> acquire sufficiently complete underlying game/play data, then EC⁸ defines its own normalization methodology.\n\nFor example, if we own:\n\nteam offensive EPA/play  \nopponent defensive EPA/play allowed  \nschedule quality  \nlocation  \ngarbage-time filtering  \nsample weighting\n\nthen Kimi eventually has enough raw evidence to define an opponent-adjustment method.\n\nThat's methodology, not Data Hound.\n\nSame with:\n\n**Loss quality / misleading final score**\n\nWe should derive that from:\n- EPA differential\n- success differential\n- turnovers\n- field position\n- red-zone performance\n- explosive differential\n- scoring-opportunity differential\n\nWe should not buy a vendor's opaque “good loss score.”\n\n### 4. POSSIBLY STRIP — if acquisition proves weak or unreliable\n\nThis is where we stay ruthless.\n\nExamples:\n\n**Turnover-worthy play rate.**  \nConceptually excellent. But if only one expensive vendor produces it, coverage is inconsistent, or methodology is opaque, then we may decide INT rate + pressure + fumbles + decision proxies are sufficient.\n\n**Proprietary OL grades.**  \nUseful, but not sacred. If we cannot reproduce or audit them, OL continuity + starts + sacks/pressure allowed + run efficiency may be better EC⁸ inputs.\n\n**Missed tackles.**  \nAgain, valuable. But if coverage is spotty or prohibitively expensive, it should not become a mandatory component field.\n\n**Detailed scheme percentages** like exact motion rate, man/zone frequency, RPO frequency. Great if available consistently. Optional if not.\n\nThat's the big philosophical shift:\n\n> **Data availability constrains methodology design. Methodology does not get to hallucinate a data layer.**\n\nAnd I want one more distinction.\n\n### 99% does not mean every conceivable football statistic\n\nOur 99% target should mean:\n\n> **99% of the data required by the locked methodology is reliably available, current, attributable and machine-usable.**\n\nNot 99% of every statistic anyone has ever invented.\n\nOtherwise we'd never finish.\n\nFor instance, the current QB profile is usable for normal statistics and continuity, but Leavitt's prior-season data is incomplete while Chambliss's is much stronger. fileciteturn2file1\n\nThat doesn't automatically mean “buy more data.”\n\nFirst question is:\n\n**Does that missing field materially affect the model?**\n\nIf yes → solve it.\n\nIf no → remove it from required schema.\n\n---\n\n### My current escalation list\n\nIf we were sitting together deciding where to spend money, these are the first things I would stop on:\n\n**RED — solve before NCAA methodology lock**\n1. Full FBS play-by-play\n2. Stable drive data\n3. Snap/player participation\n4. Pressure / pressure allowed\n5. Reliable target/route participation\n6. Historical data coverage sufficient for continuity windows\n7. Market time series at the granularity we decide we need\n\n**YELLOW — investigate before deciding**\n- missed tackles\n- havoc\n- turnover-worthy plays\n- detailed coverage metrics\n- advanced OL grades\n- motion/RPO/man-zone percentages\n\n**GREEN — already fundamentally solvable**\n- schedules\n- teams\n- coaches\n- box stats\n- basic player stats\n- rankings\n- roster/transfer continuity\n- injuries/availability\n- weather/venue\n- basic historical matchups\n- basic market state\n\nAnd we should not let a missing red item quietly become a zero in the model.\n\nMissing means **missing**.\n\nIf a component depends on it, the model either reduces confidence or stops calculation until that data requirement is satisfied.\n\nThat becomes an EC⁸-wide doctrine:\n\n> **No hidden imputation of required evidence.**\n\nThe current package already behaves correctly on this point: play-level data is explicitly `UNAVAILABLE`, opponent adjustment is `UNKNOWN`, Ole Miss OL is `PARTIAL`, and LSU special teams is `UNKNOWN`. fileciteturn1file2\n\nThat's exactly what we want the system to tell us.\n\nSo I would **not move to weighting yet**.\n\nThe next real gate is:\n\n**Required NCAA Variable → Required Data Field → Existing Source → Coverage % → Quality → Cost → Decision: KEEP / DERIVE / BUY / STRIP**\n\nOnce that matrix is complete, then we know what the true NCAA methodology is allowed to contain.\n\nSTATUS: **CHECKPOINT**  \nCONFIDENCE: **99%**  \nREASON: We now have enough evidence to distinguish genuine methodology requirements from desirable-but-unreliable data. The next decision is a source sufficiency decision, not a modeling decision.  \nNEXT GATE: **NCAA Data Requirement Matrix → API/source investigation for RED gaps → methodology retention decision**  \nDON INPUT NEEDED: **No yet. I should finish the matrix first and bring you only the fields where a real buy/strip decision is required.**", "text_chars": 8063, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "78a36712-0262-4d7a-93ec-51b4a1cb672a", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789858823.633275, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully"