ance  \nCURRENT GATE: Cross-Sport Data Sufficiency / Source Strategy\n\nUse this as the opening handoff for the new chat:\n\n```text\nEC⁸ EDGE CREW — STRUCTURAL RESEARCH HANDOFF\n\nDATE: 2026-09-19\nSESSION: Edge Crew Structural Research\nSEAT: Coda — Strategic Advisor / Governance\nCURRENT GATE: Data Sufficiency → Source Strategy → Methodology Retention\n\nPURPOSE\n\nCreate a dedicated cross-sport research thread for deciding how Edge Crew should source, derive, license, downgrade, or remove data requirements before sport methodologies are locked.\n\nThis thread is NOT for game picks and NOT for sport-specific walkthroughs.\n\nThe governing question is:\n\n“What data does Edge Crew actually need to support its methodology at production quality, and what is the most rational way to obtain it?”\n\nFOUNDATIONAL PRINCIPLE\n\nDo not keep a methodology variable merely because it is analytically attractive.\n\nFor every important variable:\n\nREQUIRED VARIABLE\n→ REQUIRED DATA FIELD\n→ CURRENT SOURCE\n→ COVERAGE %\n→ QUALITY\n→ LATENCY / TIMESTAMP QUALITY\n→ PROVENANCE\n→ COST / LICENSING\n→ DERIVABILITY\n→ DECISION\n\nAllowed decisions:\n\nKEEP\nDERIVE INTERNALLY\nBUY / LICENSE\nSUPPLEMENT\nDOWNGRADE TO OPTIONAL\nSTRIP FROM METHODOLOGY\n\nTARGET STANDARD\n\nThe objective is not “99% of every statistic that exists.”\n\nThe objective is:\n\n99%+ reliable availability for the data required by the locked Edge Crew methodology.\n\nRequired evidence should be:\n\n- current\n- attributable\n- machine-usable\n- reproducible\n- sufficiently complete\n- consistent enough for historical comparison\n- available at the latency the methodology requires\n\nNo required field should silently become zero, null-equivalent, guessed, or backfilled without explicit methodology.\n\nSOURCE CLASSES\n\nClassify every field/source as one of:\n\n1. EXISTING EC⁸ API / INTERNAL FEED\n2. PUBLIC / OFFICIAL SOURCE\n3. CHATGPT / INTEGRATED DATA SOURCE\n4. DERIVED INTERNALLY\n5. SCRAPED / RESEARCH-ACQUIRED\n6. PAID / LICENSED PROVIDER\n7. UNAVAILABLE / UNRELIABLE\n\nSOURCE LINEAGE RULE\n\nEvery durable dataset must preserve:\n\nsource_system\nsource_provider\nsource_url or source identifier\nsource_timestamp\nretrieved_timestamp\nraw_or_derived\nderivation_method if derived\nquality flag\ncoverage\nconflict state\n\nDo not mix paid, public, derived, and model-generated data without provenance.\n\nCURRENT NCAA CONTRACT TEST\n\nReference event used:\n#7 LSU at #8 Ole Miss — 2026-09-19\n\nData Hound returned a 22-CSV + markdown package covering:\n\n- games\n- team profiles\n- team-game performance\n- players\n- player-game stats\n- QB profiles\n- offensive line\n- defensive front\n- receivers\n- rushing\n- special teams\n- roster continuity\n- coaches/scheme\n- injuries/availability\n- schedule/situational\n- venue/weather\n- historical matchups\n- market snapshots\n- drive data\n- play data\n- source provenance\n- data-quality gaps\n\nKey finding:\n\nThe NCAA information model is viable, but several advanced predictive fields remain incomplete.\n\nCURRENT GREEN — FUNDAMENTALLY SOLVABLE\n\n- schedules\n- game results\n- team box stats\n- basic player stats\n- rankings\n- coaches/coordinators\n- roster/transfer history\n- broad continuity\n- injury/availability state\n- venue/weather\n- basic historical matchups\n- basic market state\n\nCURRENT RED — SOLVE BEFORE NCAA METHODOLOGY LOCK\n\n1. Full FBS play-by-play\n2. Stable drive-level data\n3. Snap / player participation\n4. Pressure / pressure allowed\n5. Reliable targets / route participation\n6. Historical coverage sufficient for continuity windows\n7. Market time-series granularity appropriate for Edge Crew\n\nCURRENT YELLOW — INVESTIGATE BEFORE RETENTION DECISION\n\n- missed tackles\n- havoc\n- turnover-worthy plays\n- detailed coverage metrics\n- advanced OL grades\n- motion rate\n- RPO rate\n- man/zone frequency\n- deeper scheme percentages\n\nIMPORTANT NCAA FINDING\n\nOpponent adjustment is currently UNKNOWN as a methodology.\n\nDo not automatically buy a provider's opponent-adjusted number.\n\nPreferred path:\n\nacquire strong raw game/play inputs\n→ define EC⁸ opponent-adjustment methodology internally\n→ preserve raw and adjusted values separately\n\nSimilarly, do not buy opaque “loss quality” or “trap game” scores.\n\nEdge Crew should derive those from defensible underlying inputs where possible.\n\nEXAMPLE INTERNAL DERIVATIONS\n\nPotentially derive:\n\n- EPA/play\n- success rate\n- explosive rate\n- early-down success\n- passing-down efficiency\n- points/drive\n- field-position value\n- scoring-opportunity efficiency\n- garbage-time filters\n- misleading-final-score indicators\n- loss quality\n- opponent-normalized performance\n\nOnly derive when the necessary raw data is reliably available.\n\nECONOMIC PRINCIPLE\n\nPaid APIs are valuable when they provide:\n\n- normalization\n- stable IDs\n- complete historical coverage\n- low-latency updates\n- bulk retrieval\n- advanced charting\n- stable schemas\n- licensing clarity\n- reduced maintenance burden\n\nDo not pay merely because a field exists somewhere behind an API.\n\nOne high-quality raw feed may replace several specialized derived feeds.\n\nCROSS-SPORT OBJECTIVE\n\nThis research thread should become sport-agnostic.\n\nFor every sport, build a matrix:\n\nSPORT\nCOMPONENT\nVARIABLE\nREQUIRED DATA\nCURRENT SOURCE\nCOVERAGE\nQUALITY\nLATENCY\nCOST\nDERIVABLE?\nPROPRIETARY?\nDECISION\nFALLBACK\nCONFIDENCE IMPACT IF MISSING\n\nThen identify common shared-service needs across:\n\nMLB\nNCAA Football\nNFL\nNBA\nWNBA\nTennis\nSoccer\nand future Edge Crew sports\n\nDO NOT ASSUME\n\n- Newer source = better source\n- Paid source = canonical source\n- One provider should own every field\n- A proprietary metric is necessary merely because professionals use it\n- Missing advanced data should automatically kill a methodology component\n\nTEST EACH GAP\n\nFor every red/yellow gap ask:\n\n1. Do we already receive this from an existing EC⁸ API?\n2. Can public/official sources supply it reliably?\n3. Can it be derived from a more fundamental feed?\n4. Is there a reputable API/provider?\n5. What does that provider actually cover?\n6. Is coverage FBS-wide / league-wide?\n7. Historical depth?\n8. Update latency?\n9. Stable identifiers?\n10. Cost?\n11. Licensing restrictions?\n12. Can we validate the field?\n13. Does the methodology materially improve enough to justify it?\n14. If not, can the variable be simplified or stripped?\n\nGOVERNANCE BOUNDARY\n\nCoda owns:\n- source-strategy governance\n- data sufficiency criteria\n- methodology retention decisions\n- cross-sport consistency\n- canon / supersession control\n\nKimi owns:\n- technical methodology\n- implementation recommendations\n- derivation design after requirements are accepted\n\nChiave owns:\n- durable architecture where materially required\n\nTalon owns:\n- deployment / sustained operations\n\nLuce owns:\n- verification / ground truth\n\nDo not allow source research to silently become architecture, implementation, or methodology lock.\n\nFIRST TASK IN THIS THREAD\n\nStart with NCAA Football RED gaps.\n\nFor each red item:\n\n- identify what data is actually required\n- identify whether our current EC⁸ stack may already contain it\n- identify credible acquisition paths\n- distinguish public vs paid vs internally derivable\n- identify likely cost/complexity class\n- recommend KEEP / DERIVE / BUY / STRIP as PROPOSED only\n- flag anything that requires Don decision\n\nDo not move to the next sport until NCAA Football red gaps are structurally understood.\n\nSTATUS: PROCEED\nCONFIDENCE: 99%\nREASON: NCAA contract testing exposed concrete data-sufficiency questions that should be solved in a dedicated cross-sport structural research thread rather than inside a live game-analysis thread.\nNEXT GATE: NCAA RED gap research → source strategy → Don decisions where needed\nDON INPUT NEEDED: No to begin\n```\n\nThat cleanly gets the source-strategy