raper instead of paying for the API.", "text_chars": 225, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "455b00e1-000f-4583-bf62-301607d6c4de", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789858938.308494, "updated_at": 1789858948.237291, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "DATE: 2026-09-19  \nSESSION: EC⁸ Edge Crew Quant — Structural Research Context  \nSEAT: Coda — Strategic Advisor / Governance  \nCURRENT GATE: Source Strategy / Historical Design Evidence\n\nThat is highly relevant context.\n\nWhat it tells me is that we already have precedent inside Edge Crew for a **hybrid acquisition model**:\n\n**paid APIs where they genuinely buy reliability or depth**  \n+  \n**custom scrapers where public data is sufficient and economically better**  \n+  \n**AI-assisted research to close specific holes**  \n+  \n**internal derivations on top of raw inputs**\n\nThe soccer scraper is especially important because it proves we should not frame every red gap as “find a paid API.” Sometimes the correct answer is:\n\n> **build the acquisition layer ourselves because the source data is public, stable enough, and cheaper to normalize internally.**\n\nThat should become part of the new Structural Research decision tree.\n\nFor every gap, the order should really be:\n\n**Existing EC⁸ feed? → Public source? → Can we scrape reliably? → Can AI-assisted research normalize it? → Can we derive it? → Only then evaluate paid API/licensed provider.**\n\nThe key caveat is production reliability. A scraper that works during discovery is not automatically equivalent to an API. We still have to evaluate source stability, robots/terms, schema drift, anti-bot exposure, update latency, historical coverage, identity resolution, and maintenance cost.\n\nSo the question isn't simply **API vs scraper**.\n\nIt's:\n\n> **Which acquisition mechanism gives us the required coverage and reliability at the lowest total operating cost?**\n\nAnd I would absolutely preserve your original Perplexity/soccer work as **historical design evidence** when we get into soccer structural research. We shouldn't rediscover a solution you've already proven.\n\nSTATUS: PROCEED  \nCONFIDENCE: 99%  \nREASON: Existing Edge Crew history demonstrates that custom acquisition can legitimately replace paid feeds in some domains, so Structural Research must evaluate BUILD alongside BUY.  \nNEXT GATE: NCAA RED gaps → EXISTING / PUBLIC / SCRAPE / DERIVE / BUY / STRIP analysis  \nDON INPUT NEEDED: No", "text_chars": 2156, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "4c9f4a8f-6430-4d04-9c4e-99304bde2ac9", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789858938.3310986, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d60870b9-8fed-489e-8cc1-1e32ac9bb55e", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789858939.6451936, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "21e7ec97-ddc5-4ae0-9ab4-7d6f6618d6a0", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789858939.6747234, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2aad8ffc-9436-44c3-8912-7ce77f0366b4", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789858939.790883, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e4832873-249c-4f4c-9878-c9812e8f8cea", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789858978.9740338, "updated_at": 1789858991.951702, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, I'm gonna give the prompt as is and then I'll talk about that and I can pull it. We could do some other things. But again, I'm all for paying APIs, but when you do fucking 16 sports and you're doing player props, the value, because we're not having anybody pay for this, needs to fucking meet the need.", "text_chars": 308, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "212d1afb-1e5f-436a-bf5b-5e082f0562b8", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789858980.673989, "updated_at": 1789858991.278762, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "DATE: 2026-09-19  \nSESSION: EC⁸ Edge Crew Quant — Structural Research Context  \nSEAT: Coda — Strategic Advisor / Governance  \nCURRENT GATE: Source Economics / Portfolio-Level Data Strategy\n\nExactly. At 16 sports plus player props, this stops being a simple “best data wins” problem and becomes a **portfolio economics problem**.\n\nThe right principle is:\n\n> **Pay when the feed buys enough unique value, reliability, scale, or latency to justify its cost across the methodology. Build or derive when the same analytical need can be met reliably for materially less. Strip the variable if neither path is rational.**\n\nA $500/month feed that solves one niche field for one sport may be a bad deal. The same $500/month feed that powers six sports, player props, historical backfill, and intraday updates may be excellent.\n\nSo in Structural Research, I would add one economic field to every source decision:\n\n**Cost per meaningful methodology requirement served**\n\nNot just subscription price.\n\nFor each API/provider/scraper we should ask:\n\n- How many sports does it cover?\n- How many required fields?\n- Does it support props?\n- Does it give historical depth?\n- Does it reduce engineering/maintenance?\n- Can one feed replace three others?\n- Does it materially improve confidence or edge?\n- What is the annual total cost?\n- What breaks if we remove it?\n\nThat gives us a more rational classification:\n\n**CORE FEED** — expensive but broadly valuable  \n**SPECIALTY FEED** — narrow but uniquely necessary  \n**BUILD INSTEAD** — public data can satisfy the need reliably  \n**DERIVE INTERNALLY** — raw feed sufficient  \n**OPTIONAL** — useful but not worth current cost  \n**STRIP** — not economically justified\n\nAnd because nobody is paying to access this right now, we should be especially disciplined about recurring vendor spend. We can always upgrade later once a source proves its value.\n\nSTATUS: PROCEED  \nCONFIDENCE: 99%  \nREASON: Cross-sport scale changes the correct optimization target from maximum data acquisition to maximum methodology value per dollar.  \nNEXT GATE: Structural Research → source-by-source utility/cost matrix  \nDON INPUT NEEDED: No", "text_chars": 2147, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "76417b3f-95fe-4602-b39c-233201f81517", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789858983.559037, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "88641235-6820-486a-9e6f-c11d4b62dee8", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789859073.870065, "updated_at": 1789859093.310183, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay. So given that we're not going to start finding data for one game, give me what happened with the data, or go ahead and reconcile first.", "text_chars": 141, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "cfb32416-cccc-440e-a00b-46bc5c30c4d5", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789859074.9077086, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e4e2943c-7cd3-4f0e-9b7c-1e294c0bea6e", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789859079.02765, "updated_at": 1789859092.861023, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "DATE: 2026-09-19  \nSESSION: EC⁸ Edge Crew Quant — NCAA Data Reconciliation  \nSEAT: Coda — Strategic Advisor / Governance  \nCURRENT GATE: Data Sufficiency → Methodology Retention\n\nI reconciled the package against the methodology. The important result is: **the NCAA data package is good enough to prove the model surface is viable, but not yet good enough to lock the full methodology.**\n\nThe package itself is structurally sound: it has 22 CSVs covering the full event/team/player/coaching/market surface, keeps market data separate from football baseline, preserves conflicts, and flags unknowns instead of inventing values. fileciteturn3file1\n\nThe strongest areas are **team performance, QB baseline, roster continuity, coaching/scheme continuity, injuries, situational context, venue/weather, recent history, and market snapshots**. LSU–Ole Miss is populated well enough in those areas to support a serious analysis. fileciteturn3file0\n\nThe weak areas are concentrated, not everywhere. The package itself identifies the main active gaps as **LSU special teams, exact Ole Miss OL five, play-level PBP/per-drive EPA, opponent-adjustment methodology, fuller market drift, and some receiving/target charting**. fileciteturn3file2\n\nThe play and drive files confirm the biggest technical gap: we currently have some **team-level EPA/success/explosive aggregates**, but not actual row-level play-by-play or true per-drive records. The LSU–Ole Miss event is explicitly marked unavailable pregame for those layers. fileciteturn3file4 fileciteturn3file6\n\nThat matters because several desirable methodology variables depend on that raw layer. If we get complete play-by-play, we can derive internally:\n\n- EPA/play\n- success rate\n- explosive rate\n- early-down efficiency\n- passing-down efficiency\n- field-position value\n- drive efficiency\n- garbage-time filtering\n- misleading final-score diagnostics\n- much of opponent normalization\n\nSo I would not buy separate APIs for each of those yet. **The first structural research target should be a complete, reliable play-by-play/participation feed.**\n\nThe next significant gap is receiving/participation detail. The receiver file is mostly `PARTIAL`: targets, target shares, YAC, and route-level information are incomplete. fileciteturn3file5\n\nCoaching/scheme, by contrast, is in surprisingly good shape. Both LSU and Ole Miss have current head coach, coordinator, continuity, staff-overlap and broad scheme information marked available. fileciteturn3file8\n\nSo the reconciliation decision is:\n\n**KEEP as required now:** core team stats, QB production/experience/availability, roster and transfer continuity, broad OL continuity, defensive production, coaching/staff continuity, injuries, schedule/situational, venue/weather, history, market state.\n\n**KEEP but source-upgrade before lock:** play-by-play, drive data, snap participation, pressure/pressure allowed, receiving targets/routes, detailed OL participation.\n\n**DERIVE internally once raw feeds exist:** EPA, success rate, explosive rates, loss quality, misleading-score indicators, opponent adjustment.\n\n**OPTIONAL unless sourcing proves cheap/reliable:** proprietary OL grades, missed tackles, turnover-worthy plays, detailed coverage grades, exact motion/RPO/man-zone rates.\n\n**STRIP if we cannot source them reliably:** any advanced field that remains sporadic or proprietary enough that it would create systematic missingness across FBS.\n\nOne more thing the reconciliation exposed: **the package has seven preserved source conflicts**, which is good governance, but means we need a source-resolution hierarchy before production. Examples include the LSU–La Tech score, Kewan Lacy’s 2025 totals, Beavers’ status, Leavitt injury characterization, and kickoff representation. fileciteturn3file2\n\nSo the next move is not “find more data for LSU–Ole Miss.”\n\nIt is:\n\n> **Take the RED source gaps into Structural Research and decide BUILD / DERIVE / BUY / STRIP at the NCAA level.**\n\nThat means we stop thinking game-by-game and solve the reusable layer.\n\nSTATUS: CHECKPOINT  \nCONFIDENCE: 99%  \nREASON: Reconciliation is complete enough to distinguish required data from optional data and identify the true source bottlenecks.  \nNEXT GATE: Structural Research → full FBS PBP / participation / pressure / target-route / market-history sourcing decisions  \nDON INPUT NEEDED: No yet. The next Don decisions should only happen when we have concrete source/cost options.", "text_chars": 4480, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3c8f39aa-fd5d-407e-9d5f-c5679abc72cf", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789859079.772889, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3fe2f857-ad31-4942-b609-6a52363732ae", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789859079.789613, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "a3fbec7d-c049-4ff7-b496-4e79590f6f23", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789859109.3019218, "updated_at": 1789859154.701408, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Agreed, but what I want to understand is now, based on the data provided, what did it influence in our first read and why?", "text_chars": 122, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "fc4a0b5d-d776-4114-964e-c5ab905608d3", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789859110.3425276, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "7f8068c1-d345-4f7a-a522-90eeac22c97a", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789859121.507047, "updated_at": 1789859152.232416, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "DATE: 2026-09-19  \nSESSION: EC⁸ Edge Crew Quant — LSU/Ole Miss Reconciliation  \nSEAT: Coda — Strategic Advisor / Governance  \nCURRENT GATE: Data Package → First-Read Reconciliation\n\nYes. This is the right question.\n\nThe clean answer is: **the package mostly strengthened the logic of our first read rather than overturning it.** It also exposed two places where our original read was too confident because the underlying data was thinner than we knew.\n\nOur first read was roughly:\n\n> **LSU fair line around -4.5**  \n> strongest LSU drivers = defense/front, depth, offensive ceiling  \n> strongest Ole Miss offsets = Chambliss continuity, home field, familiarity, LSU turnover risk  \n> uncertainty = Leavitt health + early-season sample\n\nThe package lets us now ask, component by component, whether those original beliefs were actually supported.\n\n### What got stronger\n\n**1. LSU defense/front — materially strengthened**\n\nThis was the biggest driver in the original read, and the data supports it very well.\n\nLSU came in allowing only **2.8 yards per play**, 18.5 rush yards per game, with **22 TFL and 11 sacks in two games**. Ole Miss, meanwhile, had allowed four sacks and its front had only four sacks of its own. LSU also had multiple individual front-seven impact producers already showing real production. fileciteturn5file0 fileciteturn5file1\n\nThat means the original statement—\n\n> “LSU defensive front vs Ole Miss offense is the strongest identifiable edge”\n\n—was not just narrative. The package actually **increased my confidence in that conclusion**.\n\nSo:\n\n**FIRST READ:** LSU defensive advantage  \n**DATA EFFECT:** strengthened  \n**WHY:** production, disruption, sack/TFL profile, and Ole Miss protection vulnerability all pointed the same way.\n\n---\n\n**2. Chambliss continuity — strengthened Ole Miss's offset**\n\nOur first read said Ole Miss's offense was more trustworthy than a two-game sample alone suggested.\n\nThe package confirmed that hard.\n\nChambliss had a full 2025 season behind him: **3,937 passing yards, 22 TD, 3 INT**, plus 527 rushing yards and 8 rushing TDs. In 2026 he opened 44-of-62 for 561 yards, five TD and one INT. fileciteturn5file6\n\nThat is much more important than just saying “Ole Miss started 2–0.”\n\nIt means the model should treat him as a **continuity anchor**, not a small-sample quarterback.\n\nSo:\n\n**FIRST READ:** Ole Miss QB continuity offsets LSU advantage  \n**DATA EFFECT:** materially strengthened  \n**WHY:** we now have prior-season proof that the current efficiency is structurally plausible.\n\n---\n\n**3. LSU turnover/QB error risk — strengthened**\n\nThis was also in the original read, and the data package made it clearer.\n\nLeavitt's 2026 line is **574 yards with four interceptions in two games**, while Chambliss has only one INT. fileciteturn5file6\n\nThat supports the original concern that LSU can be the better team and still fail to separate on the scoreboard because of giveaways.\n\nThat matters to spread modeling a lot.\n\nSo:\n\n**FIRST READ:** LSU's biggest self-inflicted risk is QB/turnover variance  \n**DATA EFFECT:** strengthened  \n**WHY:** the contrast with Chambliss is sharper than we initially framed it.\n\nThere is one data-quality problem here: the reference summary lists Leavitt's season total as **2 passing TDs**, but also lists the Louisiana Tech game as **4 TDs**. That is internally inconsistent, so I would not let the passing-TD figure influence the model until resolved. fileciteturn5file0\n\nThat is exactly why we built provenance/conflict handling.\n\n---\n\n**4. Coaching familiarity — became more nuanced, not more pro-LSU**\n\nOriginally we said Kiffin knowing Ole Miss was meaningful but dangerous to overvalue.\n\nThe package confirmed the overlap is extensive: **8–11 staff members and four Ole Miss players followed Kiffin to LSU**, while Ole Miss retained meaningful staff continuity under Pete Golding. fileciteturn4file9\n\nThat makes the familiarity real.\n\nBut it cuts both ways even more than we initially described.\n\nLSU knows Ole Miss.\n\nOle Miss knows Kiffin's structure.\n\nOle Miss retained John David Baker and defensive continuity pieces.\n\nTherefore the data made me **less willing to turn coaching familiarity into an LSU numerical advantage**.\n\nSo:\n\n**FIRST READ:** coaching familiarity is real but two-sided  \n**DATA EFFECT:** confirmed and made more balanced  \n**WHY:** the personnel/staff overlap exists on both sides.\n\n---\n\n**5. Historical result — became more useful, but only conditionally**\n\nThe 2025 Ole Miss win is more informative now because we can see which pieces remain relevant.\n\nOle Miss beat LSU 24–19 last year and outgained LSU **480–254**, while Chambliss threw for 314 yards. fileciteturn4file7\n\nInitially we discounted that result because LSU changed so much.\n\nThat was correct.\n\nBut the package shows that parts of that historical matchup remain useful specifically on the Ole Miss side: Chambliss, Lacy, some receiving continuity, venue familiarity, and coaching/system remnants. fileciteturn5file1\n\nSo:\n\n**FIRST READ:** recent history matters only through continuity  \n**DATA EFFECT:** strongly confirmed  \n**WHY:** the package shows exactly which participants survived.\n\nThat is probably one of the most important NCAA-methodology discoveries from this exercise.\n\n---\n\n### What weakened\n\n**6. LSU offensive-line confidence weakened slightly**\n\nOur first read gave LSU a modest roster/depth and offensive-ceiling advantage.\n\nThe package shows LSU's OL has about **91 combined career starts**, which sounds strong, but only **two starters are actually returning in place; three are new**. fileciteturn5file7\n\nThat means experience and continuity are not the same thing.\n\nWe were slightly too loose in treating LSU's talent/depth advantage as if it automatically translated to unit cohesion.\n\nSo:\n\n**FIRST READ:** LSU roster/depth slight advantage  \n**DATA EFFECT:** still plausible, but OL-specific confidence reduced  \n**WHY:** experienced transfers ≠ established unit continuity.\n\nThis is exactly the kind of distinction the schema needs.\n\n---\n\n**7. Ole Miss home/environment offset became more legitimate**\n\nWe already gave Ole Miss home-field credit.\n\nThe package adds extreme heat—roughly **97–98°F with heat index around 101°F**—plus the normal Oxford environment. fileciteturn4file6\n\nThat does not automatically mean “Ole Miss +X points.”\n\nBut it gives the venue/environment component more substance than a generic home-field number.\n\nIt especially matters for depth, defensive rotation, and sustained drives.\n\nSo:\n\n**FIRST READ:** Ole Miss home advantage  \n**DATA EFFECT:** modestly strengthened  \n**WHY:** environment is materially taxing, not just narrative.\n\n---\n\n### What the new data did NOT justify\n\n**8. It did not justify increasing the fair-line precision**\n\nThis is important.\n\nEven though some of the evidence got stronger, the package still lacks:\n\n- full play-by-play\n- true drive-level records\n- opponent-adjusted efficiency\n- pressure/pressure-allowed detail\n- complete receiving/route data\n- full LSU special-teams data fileciteturn4file4\n\nSo I would **not** say:\n\n> “We used more data, therefore LSU should now be -5.2 instead of -4.5.”\n\nThat would be false precision.\n\nThe package increased confidence in the **direction and component logic**, more than it increased confidence in the exact point estimate.\n\n---\n\n### What happened to the original fair line\n\nMy reconciliation would be:\n\n**Original:** LSU -4.5  \n**After package:** still roughly **LSU -4 to -5**\n\nI would not materially move it.\n\nBut the reason is better now.\n\nThe original LSU lean survives because:\n\n**LSU positives**\n- defensive efficiency\n- pass rush / disruption\n- run defense\n- overall offensive production\n- roster talent/depth\n- explosive offensive ceiling\n\n**Ole Miss offsets**\n- Chambliss continuity and ball security\n- experienced offensive pieces\n- home environment\n- 2025 matchup continuity\n- two-sided schematic familiarity\n- LSU OL cohesion uncertainty\n- Leavitt turnover profile\n\nThat is a much more defensible picture than what we had on the first pass.\n\n### And the market comparison became more interesting\n\nThe package shows the market actually opened around **Ole Miss -2.5** and moved all the way to **LSU -2.5/-3**. fileciteturn5file5\n\nThat matters only after the baseline.\n\nIt means the market corrected roughly **five points toward LSU** before kickoff.\n\