es not need LSU to dominate 70 plays. He may only need to identify a few predictable stress points: a QB tendency under a particular pressure look, a protection rule, a defensive coverage trigger, a personnel package, or a situational tendency.\n\nThat means the methodological unit we may have been missing is:\n\n### PROPOSED — High-Leverage Coaching Exploitation\n\nNot “coach rating.”\n\nSomething closer to:\n\n**Opponent knowledge  \n× system continuity  \n× personnel continuity  \n× tactical adaptability  \n× ability to create high-impact events**\n\nThose high-impact events are things like:\n\n- forced turnover\n- explosive pass\n- busted coverage\n- sack/fumble\n- fourth-down stop\n- red-zone tendency exploitation\n- short-field creation\n- scripted touchdown\n- successful counter after halftime\n\nAnd this is where your “21 points in three plays” example matters.\n\nI would not put **21 points** into the model as an expectation. But you're describing a **tail mechanism**:\n\n> A few correctly anticipated moments can produce a scoring swing far larger than average-down efficiency suggests.\n\nThat absolutely belongs in **Game-State / Style + Coaching / Scheme + Matchup**.\n\nThe other thing you're identifying is the difference between NFL and college execution. I would keep “players get nervous” out of baseline until we have a measurable proxy, but the broader point is defensible:\n\n**PROPOSED:** NCAA outcomes may be more sensitive to execution errors, assignment busts, and rapid momentum/game-state changes than a purely average-efficiency model captures.\n\nThat gives us another variable family:\n\n### Error-Inducement / Exploitation Interaction\n\nWe already measure the player's error profile.\n\nNow ask:\n\n> **Can this opponent specifically induce the type of error that player or unit is prone to?**\n\nExample:\n\n**QB tendency under pressure**  \n× **LSU pressure package**  \n× **Kiffin/staff knowledge of QB protection/read structure**  \n→ **elevated interception/sack/fumble risk**\n\nThat is substantially better methodology than simply saying:\n\n**QB throws interceptions = bad.**\n\nLikewise on offense:\n\n**Ole Miss coverage tendency**  \n× **Kiffin knowledge**  \n× **LSU personnel capable of attacking it**  \n→ **explosive-play opportunity**\n\nThat is the interaction.\n\nSo I think you just exposed something important for the eventual NCAA engine:\n\n> **The model cannot be only additive. It needs matchup interaction terms.**\n\nOffense 8/10 + coaching 9/10 + defense 7/10 is too crude.\n\nThe real question is:\n\n> **Does this coach/player/system specifically attack the weakness this opponent specifically presents?**\n\nThat is where an experienced coach with intimate prior knowledge could have outsized impact.\n\nThe psychological piece—young players, hostility, pressure, emotional volatility—still stays mostly in **Don Delta** until we can identify usable proxies. But the **tactical information asymmetry and high-leverage exploitation mechanism belong in baseline discovery now.**\n\nSTATUS: **PROCEED**  \nCONFIDENCE: **99% on methodology finding / uncalibrated on numerical effect**  \nREASON: The Kiffin case exposes a missing interaction layer between coaching knowledge, opponent tendencies, player error profiles, and high-leverage game events.  \nNEXT GATE: Add **Opponent-Specific Exploitation / High-Leverage Interaction** to NCAA methodology discovery and define the data needed to calculate it without narrative inflation.  \nDON INPUT NEEDED: **No**", "text_chars": 4419, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "317d1acf-98eb-435f-b8ff-9cb7b37049c0", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789859431.979635, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "p