RRENT GATE: Don Delta → High-Leverage Play Model\n\nYes. And that is the cleaner version of what I was trying to articulate:\n\n> **Football does not require you to own the entire game to own the outcome. A small number of leverage plays can create most of the scoring-margin separation.**\n\nSo your Don Delta isn't really saying **LSU is X% better across 70 snaps**.\n\nYou're asking:\n\n> **Who has the advantage on the handful of snaps most capable of deciding the game?**\n\nThat's a different analytical object.\n\nFor this matchup, your hypothesis is that there may be roughly **four or five decisive opportunities**: turnover-worthy situations, explosive-play opportunities, red-zone calls, fourth downs, pressure situations, busted assignments, major special-teams plays, etc.\n\nAnd then you're handicapping those moments separately.\n\nUsing your example, if there are five true leverage moments:\n\n- 50/50 split → essentially neutral.\n- LSU captures 3 of 5 → 60%.\n- LSU captures 4 of 5 → 80%.\n\nBut there's an important refinement: **they are not equally valuable**.\n\nA third-down stop at midfield might be worth relatively little.\n\nA strip-sack returned to the 12-yard line can create roughly a touchdown's worth of game-state swing.\n\nA busted coverage touchdown can be seven points on one snap.\n\nAn interception in your own red zone simultaneously kills expected opponent points and potentially creates your own possession.\n\nSo Don Delta eventually shouldn't just be:\n\n**5 plays × win probability.**\n\nIt becomes something more like:\n\n**Σ [Probability of leverage event × probability team wins event × expected point swing]**\n\nNow we have something potentially rigorous.\n\nAnd Kiffin's specific Don Delta would influence the middle term:\n\n**P(LSU wins leverage event | Kiffin-specific knowledge)**\n\nBaseline might see the teams approximately normally.\n\nYour Delta says:\n\n> **On the highest-information plays—the plays where knowing personnel, protections, 