ploitation** — create the matchup or mistake.  \n**Player execution/error** — whether the player responds correctly.  \n**Game management** — whether the staff handles the consequential state correctly.  \n**High-leverage conversion** — whether that moment becomes points, field position, or possession advantage.\n\nAnd yes, the “who gets the ball last” question is huge. In a close football game, **possession architecture** is itself a weapon.\n\nOne correction to our previous language: these aren't necessarily only “four or five plays.” Sometimes the decisive unit is a **sequence**—for example, timeout usage + third-down call + fourth-down decision + clock state. We should model **Leverage Events / Leverage Sequences**, not only isolated snaps.\n\nSTATUS: PROCEED  \nCONFIDENCE: 99% on framework inclusion; UNKNOWN on eventual weighting  \nREASON: Clock and timeout decisions can materially alter possession count, point expectancy, and final-game control, making them legitimate high-leverage coaching variables.  \nNEXT GATE: Build an NCAA **Leverage Event / Sequence taxonomy** including tactical exploitation, turnovers, explosives, red zone, fourth down, special teams, and clock/timeout management.  \nDON INPUT NEEDED: No", "text_chars": 3057, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "353dd738-0c13-48fc-89c8-f47692d807ad", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789859688.1446664, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "bda02a45-815a-4e3b-a9c6-2513cdf439cd", "role": "syst