irst NCAA stress test because we got **wins, losses, correct spread reads, incorrect team reads, leverage-event games, and a Don Delta thesis that partially manifested but ultimately failed**.\n\ngenuig7dd\n\n| Game | Pregame read | Final | Reconciliation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **LSU @ Ole Miss** | Baseline ~LSU -4/-5. Don: LSU by ~6; Kiffin program-builder asymmetry, halftime adjustment, leverage-play exploitation | **Ole Miss 32-24** | **MISS.** The biggest pregame error was underweighting Ole Miss's established offensive firepower and overestimating LSU's ability to support its defense. Chambliss finished with 363 passing yards; Ole Miss produced 426 yards. Kiffin *did* produce the adjustment we expected: LSU went from 24-7 down at halftime to a 24-24 tie. But Ole Miss answered and Leavitt's late end-zone INT sealed it. So the **halftime-adjustment mechanism showed up; the conclusion did not.** citeturn626864news0turn626864news7 |\n| **FSU @ Alabama** | Don/model liked FSU getting roughly +18.5/+20; Alabama could be better without deserving that margin | **Alabama 50-36** | **COVER / READ VALIDATED.** Alabama was clearly superior offensively—567 yards, 8.6 YPP—but FSU still stayed inside the huge number. Three FSU turnovers versus zero for Alabama materially inflated the margin. Perfect example of **team-quality loss but spread-value win**. citeturn626864news10turn626864news15 |\n| **SMU @ Louisville** | Model's cleanest early schema lean was SMU +2.5; Don gut was SMU by ~6 | **Louisville 41-31** | **MISS.** But this game gave us one of the day's best methodology lessons. SMU actually had more total yards, 479-442, yet lost the turnover battle **3-0** and Louisville created far more explosive efficiency at 7.4 YPP. Louisville also ripped the decisive 53-yard TD after SMU had gone ahead 31-28. This is precisely why **leverage events + turnovers + explosiveness cannot be buried inside raw yardage.** citeturn626864news17turn626864news22 |\n| **Kentucky @ Texas A&M** | Model liked Kentucky +16.5/+17. Don later agreed, but that Don read came after kickoff and is contaminated | **Kentucky 31-21 outright** | **MODEL STRONGLY VALIDATED; DON NOT CREDITED PROSPECTIVELY.** A&M ran 84 plays and gained 423 yards, but Kentucky generated 408 on only 54 plays—**7.6 YPP vs 5.0**—and won turnovers 2-0. This was exactly the **perception/brand inflation** type of case we wanted to detect. citeturn626864news32turn626864news38 |\n| **USC @ Rutgers** | Model leaned Rutgers +22.5. Don leaned USC because Rutgers knowledge was thin | **USC 42-35** | **MODEL RIGHT / DON WRONG.** Rutgers nearly matched USC yard-for-yard, 460-461, and had more possession. USC got two takeaways, including a pick-six, yet still won by only seven. This validates the **favorite-inflation / backdoor-resistance** concept and demonstrates why Don needs the baseline/player context auto-populated before giving a read. citeturn626864news40turn626864news41 |\n| **Michigan State @ Notre Dame** | Don liked Michigan State at roughly +29.5: Notre Dame insufficiently proven, huge number against a major program, possible possession compression | **Notre Dame 27-10** | **THESIS VALIDATED, but retrospective evidence only.** Notre Dame was clearly better—395-167 yards and 37 minutes of possession—but still only won by 17. Even with two Michigan State turnovers, Notre Dame never approached the required 30-point separation. That is almost a textbook **Margin Sustainability** case. citeturn626864news25turn626864news29 |\n| **Utah State @ Utah** | Pass. Model's tiny dog lean was not strong enough to matter | **Utah 33-0** | **PASS DISCIPLINE VALIDATED.** Any slight Utah State value thesis was wrong, but we didn't force the play. This reinforces your broader philosophy: there are too many games across too many sports to chase marginal edges. |\n\n### LSU–OLE MISS: THE MOST IMPORTANT RECONCILIATION\n\nThis was the centerpiece, and we need to own exactly what happened