w Quant — NCAA Postmortem / Don Delta Governance  \nSEAT: Coda — Strategic Advisor / Governance  \nCURRENT GATE: Don Delta Process Design\n\nYes. That is probably the biggest process correction from yesterday.\n\nThe failure was not that you had an LSU gut lean. The failure was that we did not force a **two-sided Don Delta interrogation** before locking the human layer.\n\nYour lean should be allowed to exist. But the process has to challenge it.\n\nSo the Don Delta should not be:\n\n> **Don leans LSU → find reasons LSU can win.**\n\nIt should be:\n\n> **Don leans LSU → build the strongest LSU case → build the strongest Ole Miss case → identify what would invalidate each side → then reconcile the net Delta.**\n\nThat is the guardrail.\n\nFor LSU–Ole Miss, if we had done that properly, the Ole Miss side would have forced us to confront:\n\n- Chambliss was much more proven than your gut read assumed.\n- Lacy was a real high-end weapon.\n- Ole Miss had more current offensive continuity.\n- Home field was meaningful.\n- LSU was still early in Kiffin’s installation cycle.\n- Leavitt had meaningful shakiness/turnover risk.\n- Ole Miss’s familiarity with Kiffin was real, even if you weighted it less.\n\nThat does not necessarily mean we would have flipped off LSU. But it almost certainly would have **reduced the strength of the Don Delta** and widened uncertainty.\n\nSo I want to formalize this:\n\n## PROPOSED DON DELTA PROCESS\n\n**1. Initial Don Lean**  \nRaw instinct, before debate.\n\n**2. Pro-Lean Case**  \nWhat supports it?\n\n**3. Counter-Case**  \nWhat is the strongest evidence for the other side?\n\n**4. Failure Conditions**  \nWhat would make the Don thesis wrong?\n\n**5. Asymmetry Check**  \nAre we overweighting information because we know one side better?\n\n**6. Reconciled Don Delta**  \nNet human adjustment after both sides are pressure-tested.\n\n**7. Confidence / Uncertainty**  \nHow sure are we after the counter-case?\n\nThat should be **mandatory per sport**.\n\nAnd there is another rule coming out of this:\n\n> **Don familiarity with one team must never be mistaken for superior information about the matchup.**\n\nYesterday, your familiarity was stronger with Kiffin than with the actual Ole Miss personnel. That is exactly the kind of asymmetry the process must expose.\n\nSo the system should explicitly ask:\n\n**What do you know about the other side that could beat your read?**\n\nThat one question would have helped materially yesterday.\n\nSTATUS: PROCEED  \nCONFIDENCE: 99%  \nREASON: Two-sided Don Delta interrogation is necessary to prevent confirmation bias and familiarity bias from contaminating the human layer.  \nNEXT GATE: Lock a sport-agnostic **Don Delta Counter-Case Protocol**, then specialize the prompts by sport.  \nDON INPUT NEEDED: No", "text_chars": 2774, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f9e52ef0-bba3-4e42-8461-e6e4891034d7", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789919912.870496, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "b5f3cd47-be4c-4b04-99c6-43cd41773a3e", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789919964.317991, "updated_at": 1789919977.358867, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "And again, because I reached the conversation limit yesterday, we weren't able to talk about it. Talking about it is like the key with me, right? Having a conversation is when I go, Aha, I may be wrong. Readin