5029, "updated_at": 1789924414.034134, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "DATE: 2026-09-20  \nSESSION: EC⁸ Edge Crew Quant — NCAA Data Expansion  \nSEAT: Coda — Strategic Advisor / Governance  \nCURRENT GATE: NCAA Data Sufficiency → Source Discovery / Acquisition Strategy\n\nYes. Now we push the Hound harder.\n\nThe existing NCAA package proved that we can populate most of the framework. This prompt is **not asking him to collect another game's data**. It asks him to attack the remaining methodology requirements at the **FBS-wide production level** and tell us exactly where the data can come from, whether we should scrape it, derive it, buy it, combine sources, or strip the variable.\n\nCopy this as-is:\n\n```text\nEC⁸ EDGE CREW QUANT — NCAA FOOTBALL STRUCTURAL DATA HOUND PROMPT\nSOURCE DISCOVERY / GAP CLOSURE / BUILD-vs-BUY-vs-DERIVE\n\nDATE: 2026-09-20\nSPORT: NCAA FOOTBALL / FBS\nMODE: STRUCTURAL RESEARCH\nPURPOSE: Determine how EC⁸ can support the emerging NCAA Football methodology at production quality across the full FBS universe.\n\nTHIS IS NOT A SINGLE-GAME DATA COLLECTION TASK.\n\nDo not research LSU–Ole Miss specifically except when using the existing LSU–Ole Miss package as a reference for fields, coverage, provenance, or known gaps.\n\nWe already completed an initial NCAA data package and live methodology stress test.\n\nYour job now is to push the limits of the sourcing problem.\n\n==================================================\nGOVERNING QUESTION\n==================================================\n\nFor every important NCAA Football methodology requirement:\n\nWHAT DATA DO WE ACTUALLY NEED?\n→ DOES EC⁸ ALREADY HAVE IT?\n→ IS IT PUBLICLY AVAILABLE?\n→ CAN WE SCRAPE IT RELIABLY?\n→ CAN WE DERIVE IT FROM MORE FUNDAMENTAL DATA?\n→ IS THERE A FREE / LOW-COST SOURCE?\n→ IS THERE A PAID API OR LICENSED PROVIDER?\n→ WHAT DOES THAT PROVIDER ACTUALLY BUY US?\n→ WHAT IS COVERAGE?\n→ WHAT IS HISTORY?\n→ WHAT IS LATENCY?\n→ WHAT IS QUALITY?\n→ WHAT IS MAINTENANCE BURDEN?\n→ WHAT IS LEGAL / LICENSING RISK?\n→ WHAT IS THE FALLBACK?\n→ SHOULD THE VARIABLE REMAIN IN THE METHODOLOGY?\n\nDO NOT assume paid API = best answer.\n\nDO NOT assume scraping = best answer.\n\nDO NOT assume a variable should remain merely because it sounds analytically useful.\n\n==================================================\nTARGET STANDARD\n==================================================\n\nEC⁸ target:\n\n99%+ reliable availability for DATA REQUIRED BY THE LOCKED METHODOLOGY.\n\nThat means:\n\n- current\n- attributable\n- machine-usable\n- reproducible\n- historically consistent enough for modeling\n- stable IDs where practical\n- sufficient FBS-wide coverage\n- timestamped appropriately\n- no hidden guessing\n- no silent zero-fill\n- no narrative substitution for missing structured evidence\n\nIf a required variable cannot realistically meet that standard:\n\nFLAG IT.\n\nPossible decisions:\n\nKEEP\nDERIVE INTERNALLY\nBUILD / SCRAPE\nBUY / LICENSE\nSUPPLEMENT / MULTI-SOURCE\nDOWNGRADE TO OPTIONAL\nSTRIP FROM METHODOLOGY\n\n==================================================\nEXISTING NCAA PACKAGE — WHAT WE ALREADY KNOW\n==================================================\n\nThe first NCAA package successfully covered much of:\n\n- games\n- team profiles\n- team-game performance\n- players\n- player game stats\n- QB profiles\n- broad OL information\n- defensive fronts\n- receivers\n- rushing\n- special teams\n- roster continuity\n- coaching / scheme\n- injuries / availability\n- schedule / situational\n- venue / weather\n- historical matchups\n- market snapshots\n- source provenance\n- quality / gap reporting\n\nThe package proved that the broad information model is viable.\n\nDo not rebuild those layers blindly.\n\nInstead investigate what is missing, partial, inconsistent, expensive, or inadequately scalable.\n\n==================================================\nPRIMARY RED GAPS\n==================================================\n\nThese are the first problems to attack.\n\n1. FULL FBS PLAY-BY-PLAY\n\nNeed to know:\n\n- complete FBS coverage?\n- current season?\n- historical seasons?\n- real-time or postgame?\n- play clock / quarter / down / distance?\n- yard line?\n- possession?\n- play type?\n- passer/rusher/receiver IDs?\n- penalties?\n- turnovers?\n- sacks?\n- field goals?\n- punts?\n- kickoffs?\n- scoring?\n- timeout events?\n- reviews?\n- drive IDs?\n- garbage-time identification inputs?\n\nFind every credible source.\n\nInvestigate:\n\n- official / NCAA\n- conference/team gamebooks\n- ESPN/public endpoints if legitimately accessible\n- CFBD / CollegeFootballData\n- Sportradar\n- SportsDataIO\n- Stats Perform / Opta\n- Genius Sports\n- other credible vendors\n- open-source repositories\n- GitHub projects\n- community-maintained feeds\n- scraping possibilities\n- historical archives\n\nFor each:\ncoverage, history, latency, cost, licensing, reliability, schema stability, IDs, rate limits, maintenance burden.\n\nAsk specifically:\n\nCAN WE BUILD OUR OWN NORMALIZED FBS PBP PIPELINE WITHOUT BUYING AN EXPENSIVE PREMIUM FEED?\n\nIf yes, explain precisely how.\n\n==================================================\n\n2. DRIVE DATA\n\nDetermine whether true drive records are available directly or should be derived from PBP.\n\nRequired concepts:\n\n- drive start/end\n- field position\n- plays\n- yards\n- duration\n- result\n- points\n- turnover\n- explosive plays\n- scoring opportunity\n- three-and-out\n- red-zone entry\n- starting field position\n\nQuestion:\n\nSHOULD EC⁸ BUY DRIVE DATA OR DERIVE IT FROM PBP?\n\nShow the evidence and recommendation.\n\n==================================================\n\n3. PLAYER PARTICIPATION / SNAP COUNTS\n\nWe need to know whether we can reliably obtain:\n\n- offensive snaps\n- defensive snaps\n- special-team snaps\n- starts\n- substitution / participation\n- position-level participation\n- OL snap continuity\n- QB snaps\n- skill-position usage\n\nInvestigate public, scraped and paid sources.\n\nEspecially determine:\n\n- Is NCAA participation sufficiently standardized?\n- Are official gamebooks usable?\n- Can gamebook PDFs / HTML be parsed systematically?\n- Is there a public source already normalizing this?\n- Is PFF effectively the only strong source?\n- Are there alternatives?\n\nGive realistic FBS-wide coverage percentages.\n\n==================================================\n\n4. PRESSURE / PASS PROTECTION\n\nRequired methodological concepts may include:\n\n- QB pressures\n- pressure rate\n- sacks\n- sack responsibility if possible\n- pressures allowed\n- pass-block performance\n- pocket disruption\n- blitz pressure\n- pressure by defensive player\n- pressure by OL / protection unit\n\nInvestigate:\n\n- what is observable publicly\n- what can be derived\n- what requires charting\n- what requires PFF / premium vendor\n- whether sacks + PBP + play context are sufficient proxies\n- whether EC⁸ should create its own pressure proxy instead of buying proprietary grades\n\nDo not recommend proprietary metrics merely because they exist.\n\n==================================================\n\n5. TARGET / ROUTE / RECEIVER PARTICIPATION\n\nNeed to investigate:\n\n- targets\n- target share\n- routes run\n- route participation\n- air yards\n- depth of target\n- YAC\n- explosive receptions\n- receiving alignment if available\n- drop information\n- contested targets if available\n\nSeparate:\n\nPUBLICLY AVAILABLE\nDERIVABLE\nCHARTING-DEPENDENT\nPROPRIETARY\n\nTell us which fields are realistic methodology requirements.\n\n==================================================\n\n6. HISTORICAL CONTINUITY\n\nNCAA requires continuity-aware evidence.\n\nInvestigate how we can build reliable history for:\n\n- player seasons\n- prior schools\n- transfers\n- coaching tenure\n- coordinator tenure\n- coaching trees / staff overlap\n- returning production\n- OL continuity\n- QB experience\n- starter continuity\n- roster turnover\n- portal additions/losses\n- scheme continuity\n\nDetermine where stable historical IDs are available and where entity resolution must be built internally.\n\n==================================================\n\n7. MARKET TIME SERIES\n\nWe need more than one current line.\n\nInvestigate sources for:\n\n- opener\n- timestamped line movement\n- spread\n- total\n- moneyline\n- multiple books\n- consensus\n- closing line\n- historical closing lines\n- potentially derivative markets later\n\nWe may already have sports-odds APIs elsewhere in EC⁸.\n\nDO NOT recommend new vendors until checking whether existing EC⁸ sources could satisfy this requirement.\n\nCompare likely providers only where necessary.\n\n==================================================\nNEW METHODOLOGY REQUIREMENTS DISCOVERED IN LIVE TEST\n==================================================\n\nOur live NCAA stress test exposed additional requirements.\n\nInvestigate the DATA necessary to support them.\n\nDo not invent scores for these concepts.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nA. OPPONENT QUALITY / DYNAMIC SCHEDULE STRENGTH\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nWe discovered that program reputation can badly distort human interpretation.\n\nExample pattern:\n\n\"Beat Clemson\" cannot automatically mean high-quality win if current Clemson is materially weaker than brand reputation suggests.\n\nNeed raw inputs capable of supporting:\n\n- opponent-adjusted efficiency\n- current opponent strength\n- recursive strength of schedule\n- offensive opponent quality\n- defensive opponent quality\n- temporal weighting\n- current-season small-sample handling\n\nQuestion:\n\nWhat fundamental data do we need so EC⁸ can derive its OWN opponent adjustment rather than purchasing an opaque adjusted number?\n\nResearch established methodologies for inspiration, but do not copy proprietary ratings.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nB. LOSS QUALITY / MISLEADING FINAL SCORE\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nSaturday repeatedly demonstrated that final score alone is inadequate.\n\nNeed raw data capable of distinguishing:\n\n- competitive underlying game\n- garbage-time separation\n- turnover-driven margin\n- explosive-play-driven margin\n- field-position distortion\n- red-zone variance\n- late-game score effects\n- possessions\n- success rate\n- yards/play\n- scoring opportunities\n\nDetermine what data is required for EC⁸ to derive:\n\nLOSS QUALITY\nMISLEADING FINAL SCORE\nWIN QUALITY\n\ninternally.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nC. MARGIN SUSTAINABILITY\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nThis is important.\n\nThe human layer repeatedly identified large underdogs where the favorite could be clearly superior but still unlikely to justify the market margin.\n\nExamples from the first stress test included:\n\n- Wake Forest vs Miami\n- Florida State vs Alabama\n- Michigan State vs Notre Dame\n- Kentucky vs Texas A&M\n\nWe need data capable of answering:\n\n\"Can the favorite realistically create and sustain the possession and scoring separation required by this spread?\"\n\nInvestigate fields useful for:\n\n- expected possessions\n- pace\n- run rate\n- explosive-play rate\n- three-and-out rate\n- scoring drives\n- points/drive\n- opponent points/drive\n- garbage-time scoring\n- late-game tempo\n- favorite ability to extend leads\n- underdog ability to shorten games\n- fourth-quarter scoring behavior\n- backdoor-cover profile\n- kneel / clock-kill situations\n\nQuestion:\n\nCan these be derived primarily from PBP?\n\nIf yes, specify the derivation architecture.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nD. LEVERAGE EVENTS / LEVERAGE SEQUENCES\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nWe discovered that football may be decided by a handful of highly consequential events even when neither team dominates every snap.\n\nPotential events:\n\n- interceptions\n- fumbles\n- sacks / strip sacks\n- explosive touchdowns\n- fourth-down attempts\n- fourth-down stops\n- red-zone conversion\n- goal-line sequences\n- blocked kicks\n- punt/kick return events\n- missed field goals\n- end-of-half possessions\n- timeout management\n- two-minute sequences\n- short fields\n- sudden-change possessions\n- halftime tactical adjustments\n- late-game possession control\n\nInvestigate what raw data is necessary to identify and value these events.\n\nCan EC⁸ derive an EXPECTED POINT SWING or WIN-PROBABILITY SWING for these situations?\n\nWhat data would that require?\n\nDo not build the model yet.\n\nTell us whether the necessary data can realistically be sourced.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nE. COACHING / GAME MANAGEMENT\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nWe need more than head coach name.\n\nInvestigate whether historical data can support:\n\n- fourth-down aggressiveness\n- timeout usage\n- clock management\n- end-of-half decision making\n- late-game decision making\n- two-point conversion decisions\n- field-goal / punt / go decisions\n- pace changes by game state\n- halftime performance changes\n- scripted-drive performance\n- post-halftime drive performance\n\nQuestion:\n\nCan EC⁸ derive meaningful coach-level behavioral profiles from PBP?\n\nIf yes:\nwhat history is needed?\nhow many games?\nwhat fields?\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nF. SYSTEM MATURITY / INSTALLATION AGE\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nLSU–Ole Miss exposed:\n\nCOACH QUALITY != SYSTEM MATURITY.\n\nWe need factual inputs such as:\n\n- new HC?\n- new OC/DC?\n- year in system\n- QB year in system\n- returning starters\n- transfer percentage\n- returning OL starts\n- returning skill production\n- staff continuity\n- player/staff migration\n- coordinator continuity\n\nInvestigate how these can be sourced FBS-wide.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nG. HOME-FIELD / ENVIRONMENT QUALITY\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nDo not reduce home field to a generic constant.\n\nInvestigate data for:\n\n- historical home performance\n- attendance\n- stadium capacity\n- travel distance\n- time zones\n- altitude\n- temperature\n- heat index\n- wind\n- precipitation\n- surface\n- kickoff time\n- night/day\n- rivalry context where objectively identifiable\n\nWe do NOT need fake \"crowd intensity scores.\"\n\nWe need factual inputs from which methodology may later derive effects.\n\n==================================================\nDON DELTA SUPPORT DATA\n==================================================\n\nThe Don Delta remains separate from baseline.\n\nHowever, the system should eventually AUTO-POPULATE factual context before Don gives a read.\n\nThe LSU–Ole Miss postmortem showed why.\n\nDon underweighted:\n\n- Chambliss\n- Lacy\n- Ole Miss continuity\n- home-field context\n\nand knew Kiffin better than he knew LSU's current player personnel.\n\nWe therefore need a PRE-DON CONTEXT PACKAGE that automatically surfaces:\n\nTEAM A:\n- key QB\n- RB\n- primary receivers\n- OL continuity\n- defensive stars / disruption\n- current injuries\n- coaching/staff continuity\n- current opponent-adjusted form\n- recent schedule quality\n\nTEAM B:\nsame.\n\nThen:\n\n- strongest baseline case for A\n- strongest baseline case for B\n- critical unknowns\n- key leverage matchup candidates\n\nDO NOT create Don's opinion.\n\nGive him the factual board necessary to have the conversation.\n\n==================================================\nSOURCE ECONOMICS\n==================================================\n\nEC⁸ may eventually cover roughly 16 sports plus player props.\n\nTherefore every source decision must consider portfolio economics.\n\nFor each paid provider include:\n\n- monthly cost\n- annual cost\n- sport coverage\n- NCAA coverage\n- historical depth\n- live latency\n- player-level depth\n- prop support if applicable\n- stable IDs\n- API limits\n- commercial/use restrictions\n- whether one subscription replaces multiple feeds\n- engineering hours saved\n- unique fields we cannot obtain elsewhere\n\nCalculate where possible:\n\nCOST PER MEANINGFUL METHODOLOGY REQUIREMENT SERVED\n\nClassify:\n\nCORE FEED\nSPECIALTY FEED\nBUILD INSTEAD\nDERIVE INTERNALLY\nOPTIONAL\nSTRIP\n\nWe are willing to pay for APIs.\n\nWe are NOT willing to accumulate expensive subscriptions across 16 sports merely because buying is easier than thinking.\n\n==================================================\nSCRAPER / BUILD INVESTIGATION\n==================================================\n\nBe aggressive here.\n\nFor each relevant public source ask:\n\n- Is scraping technically feasible?\n- Is the structure stable?\n- HTML, JSON, embedded endpoint, PDF?\n- update timing?\n- historical access?\n- rate-limit exposure?\n- anti-bot exposure?\n- terms/licensing concern?\n- maintenance expectation?\n- FBS-wide scalability?\n- stable IDs?\n- entity-resolution requirement?\n\nWe previously built a soccer scraper instead of paying for an unnecessary API.\n\nThat is valid precedent.\n\nBut do not recommend a scraper when the source is unstable enough that API economics clearly win.\n\n==================================================\nDELIVERABLE 1 — NCAA SOURCE MATRIX\n==================================================\n\nCreate a structured matrix:\n\nMETHOD COMPONENT\nVARIABLE\nREQUIRED FIELD(S)\nCURRENT EC⁸ SOURCE IF KNOWN\nPUBLIC SOURCE OPTIONS\nSCRAPER OPTION\nDERIVABLE?\nPAID PROVIDER OPTIONS\nCOVERAGE %\nHISTORICAL DEPTH\nLATENCY\nDATA QUALITY\nID QUALITY\nMAINTENANCE BURDEN\nLICENSE / TERMS CONCERN\nAPPROX COST\nFALLBACK\nRECOMMENDED DECISION\nCONFIDENCE\nNOTES\n\n==================================================\nDELIVERABLE 2 — PROVIDER COMPARISON\n==================================================\n\nCompare credible NCAA providers.\n\nAt minimum investigate, if applicable:\n\nCollegeFootballData / CFBD\nSportradar\nSportsDataIO\nStats Perform / Opta\nGenius Sports\nPFF\nThe Odds API or existing EC⁸ odds providers\nofficial NCAA / schools / conferences\ncredible public alternatives\n\nDO NOT assume all listed providers are relevant.\n\nRemove irrelevant ones.\n\nAdd better candidates if discovered.\n\n==================================================\nDELIVERABLE 3 — BUILD / DERIVE ARCHITECTURE\n==================================================\n\nIdentify what EC⁸ should plausibly create internally from fundamental feeds.\n\nPotential derived layers:\n\nEPA/play\nsuccess rate\nexplosive rate\nearly-down efficiency\npassing-down efficiency\npoints/drive\nfield-position value\nscoring opportunities\ngarbage-time filters\nturnover-adjusted performance\nloss quality\nwin quality\nmisleading final score\nopponent adjustment\nmargin sustainability\nbackdoor-cover profile\ncoach decision profiles\nleverage-event value\n\nFor each:\n\nRAW INPUT REQUIRED\nFORMULA / METHODOLOGY SOURCE OPTIONS\nHISTORICAL TRAINING NEED\nVALIDATION METHOD\nEXPECTED COVERAGE\nLIMITATIONS\n\nDo NOT implement calculations yet.\n\nThis is source/methodology feasibility research.\n\n==================================================\nDELIVERABLE 4 — STRIP LIST\n==================================================\n\nBe willing to kill variables.\n\nCreate:\n\nVARIABLE\nWHY ANALYTICALLY ATTRACTIVE\nWHY DATA IS INADEQUATE / TOO EXPENSIVE / TOO OPAQUE\nPOSSIBLE PROXY\nCONFIDENCE LOSS IF REMOVED\nRECOMMENDATION\n\nPay special attention to:\n\nturnover-worthy plays\nproprietary OL grades\nmissed tackles\ncoverage grades\nmotion %\nRPO %\nman/zone %\nroute-level charting\npressure attribution\n\nIf they are worth the cost, prove it.\n\nIf they are not, say so.\n\n==================================================\nDELIVERABLE 5 — PRIORITIZED ACQUISITION PLAN\n==================================================\n\nReturn:\n\nTIER 1 — MUST SOLVE BEFORE NCAA METHODOLOGY LOCK\nTIER 2 — HIGH VALUE BUT CAN FOLLOW\nTIER 3 — NICE TO HAVE\nTIER 4 — DO NOT PURSUE\n\nFor each Tier 1 item provide:\n\nBEST CURRENT OPTION\nSECOND OPTION\nBUILD OPTION\nPAID OPTION\nCOST RANGE\nIMPLEMENTATION COMPLEXITY\nEXPECTED COVERAGE\nPRIMARY RISK\nRECOMMENDED NEXT ACTION\n\n==================================================\nNON-NEGOTIABLE RULES\n==================================================\n\n- No picks.\n- No betting recommendations.\n- No fair line.\n- No edge calculation.\n- No EV calculation.\n- No Don Delta opinion.\n- No methodology weighting.\n- No invented data.\n- No silent conflict resolution.\n- No \"probably available\" without identifying the actual source.\n- No recommendation to purchase before checking public, existing, scraped, and derived alternatives.\n- No keeping a variable merely because professionals commonly use it.\n- Separate FACT from INFERENCE from PROPOSAL.\n- Preserve provenance.\n\n==================================================\nFINAL QUESTION\n==================================================\n\nAt the end, answer this directly:\n\n\"If EC⁸ wanted a production-quality NCAA Football engine with approximately 99% availability of REQUIRED methodology data, what is the smallest, smartest combination of:\n\nEXISTING EC⁸ DATA\n+ PUBLIC DATA\n+ CUSTOM ACQUISITION / SCRAPERS\n+ INTERNALLY DERIVED METRICS\n+ PAID / LICENSED FEEDS\n\nthat would realistically get us there?\"\n\nWe are optimizing for:\n\nANALYTICAL QUALITY\n× RELIABILITY\n× COVERAGE\n× MAINTAINABILITY\n÷ TOTAL COST\n\nNot maximum vendor spend.\n\nReturn unresolved questions explicitly for Coda / Don review.\n```\n\nThat is the prompt I would run now.\n\nThe big change from the first Hound mission is that we're no longer asking **“can you get this stat?”** We're asking:\n\n> **“Show me the cheapest reliable chain from raw evidence to the methodology requirement—and prove when buying is actually smarter than building.”**\n\nAnd I've deliberately pushed **margin sustainability** hard in there because yesterday gave us enough repeated evidence that it deserves structural investigation: Wake, FSU, Michigan State and Kentucky were not the same football situations, but they all exposed the same underlying question—**does the favorite actually possess the game structure required to create the market's demanded margin?**\n\nSTATUS: PROCEED  \nCONFIDENCE: 99%  \nREASON: The initial NCAA package established viability; the next problem is no longer collection for a reference game but production-scale source economics and derivability.  \nNEXT GATE: Hound returns NCAA source matrix → Coda reconciles KEEP / BUILD / DERIVE / BUY / STRIP before methodology lock.  \nDON INPUT NEEDED: No", "text_chars": 21701, "code_blocks": 1, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "9165a462-2e0f-4af6-89b2-47c370896431", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789924334.5644085, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "8d647f04-ef60-4475-b874-b34931953898", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789924336.8250794, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "23ba30e0-b7d0-4815-9c2e-254d