 / Governance  \nCURRENT GATE: Outcome Reconciliation → Exogenous Disruption\n\nYes. That materially changes how we should grade the FSU–Alabama read.\n\nFSU was up **21–13 when the weather delay hit**, and the game then had a delay of roughly two and a half hours. After play resumed, Alabama took control and eventually won 50–36.\n\nThat means we should not treat the final score as if it arose from one uninterrupted football process.\n\nThis gives us another methodology distinction:\n\n> **Exogenous interruption ≠ team-performance failure.**\n\nWeather delays, lightning, evacuation, extended halftime-like stoppages, field-condition changes, and restart effects can materially alter rhythm, preparation, physiology, crowd state, and game-state momentum.\n\nAnd importantly, those are **not reasonably predictable in the same way as ordinary weather conditions**.\n\nWe can model pregame weather risk.\n\nWe cannot pretend we predicted:\n\n**“FSU will be leading, then there will be a 2.5-hour stoppage, then Alabama will reset and flip the game.”**\n\nSo the correct reconciliation is:\n\n**Pregame FSU +points thesis:** validated by the cover.\n\n**Underlying football read before interruption:** also looked strong; FSU was leading and had established control.\n\n**Post-delay performance:** Alabama dominated enough to win comfortably.\n\n**Causal attribution:** UNKNOWN. We cannot say the delay caused the reversal just because it happened before it. But it is a material state discontinuity and absolutely belongs in the postgame record.\n\nThat suggests a new field for reconciliation:\n\n### EXOGENOUS GAME-STATE DISRUPTION\n\nExamples:\n- lightning/weather delay\n- field evacuation\n- major injury delay\n- equipment/lighting failure\n- unusual officiating stoppage\n- field-condition change\n- venue interruption\n\nThen classify:\n\n**NONE / MINOR / MATERIAL / SEVERE**\n\nand record:\n\n**timing → score → possession → duration → state before → state after**\n\nThat way loss-quality and model verification don't blindly treat the game as continuous.\n\nAnd you're right about the larger point: **this is exactly why a model can be “right” about a side without correctly predicting the exact path of the game.** There is irreducible variance.\n\nSTATUS: CHECKPOINT  \nCONFIDENCE: 99% on classification; UNKNOWN on causal effect of the delay  \nREASON: The extended weather stoppage was a material exogenous disruption occurring while FSU held the lead, so postgame evaluation should preserve it without claiming it caused Alabama's comeback.  \nNEXT GATE: Add **Exogenous Game-State Disruption** to NCAA reconciliation and loss-quality schema.  \nDON INPUT NEEDED: No", "text_chars": 2732, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "08f119f5-b213-420d-9db2-f65ca6bf795f", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789924419.1559198, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "7636725c-fa46-4c19-be8b-ccf09b61823a", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789924421.4903517, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2771f379-b668-4519-a638-3655a39a5be1", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789924421.493419